he table above." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247196 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6466b906-2338-59f9-b490-f79a50950cf6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247196 #edb/ref 1074 "Using the values in the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=2) & =P((X,Y)\\in {(2,2),(2,3)}) \\\\  & =P((X,Y)=(2,2))+P((X,Y)=(2,3)) \\\\  & =f(2,2)+f(2,3) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247200 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246926 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247200 #edb/ref 1069 "q-111076" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247200 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247200 #edb/ref 1071 "|  | $Y=1$ | $Y=3$ | $Y=5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=2$ | $\\frac{1}{6}$ | $\\frac{1}{6}$ | $\\frac{2}{9}$ |\n| $X=4$ | $\\frac{1}{9}$ | $\\frac{2}{9}$ | $\\frac{1}{9}$ |\n\nCompute $P(X=4\\,\\,\\text{or}\\,\\,Y>4)$ given that the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247200 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ed526ede-6ba9-5979-b07c-e565cdc8a6e2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247200 #edb/ref 1074 "Using the values in the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=4\\,\\,\\text{or}\\,\\,Y>4) & =P((X,Y)\\in {(2,5),(4,1),(4,3),(4,5)}) \\\\  & =P((X,Y)=(2,5))+P((X,Y)=(4,1)) \\\\  & +P((X,Y)=(4,3))+P((X,Y)=(4,5)) \\\\  & =f(2,5)+f(4,1)+f(4,3)+f(4,5) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}+\\frac{1}{9}+\\frac{2}{9}+\\frac{1}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247258 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246970 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247258 #edb/ref 1069 "q-112325" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247258 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247258 #edb/ref 1071 "Use Pascal's triangle to determine the value of $(\\frac{3}{3}).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247258 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f22cfc7d-e4da-562f-9d3d-9e3613fd5a63" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247258 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the value of $(\\frac{3}{3}),$ we need to find the number that is located in row $3$ and position $3$ of Pascal's triangle. \n\n**Important**: When counting the rows and positions in Pascal's triangle, we always start counting from zero! Remember:\n\n- The top row of Pascal's triangle is row $0.$\n\n- The first position of each row (the one of the left) is position number $0.$\n\nThe value located in row $3$ and position $3$ is highlighted below.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  &  &  &  &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  &  &  & 1 &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  &  & 1 &  & 2 &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  & 1 &  & 3 &  & 3 &  & 1 \\\\  &  & 1 &  & 4 &  & 6 &  & 4 &  & 1 \\\\  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $(\\frac{3}{3})=1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247286 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246962 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247286 #edb/ref 1069 "q-112561" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247286 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247286 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $(x,y)$ be the solution of the following system. What is the value of $x+y?$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+3y=7 \\\\ 0.5x+0.4y=0.2\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247286 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "67d9ca65-c314-5c48-b66e-c63d3eedec32" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247286 #edb/ref 1074 "To get rid of the decimals, we multiply the second equation by $10,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+3y=7 &  \\\\ 0.5x+0.4y=0.2 & |\\,\\times 10\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}x+3y=7 \\\\ 5x+4y=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that if we multiply the  first equation by $-5,$ then we will get a $-5x$ term that can cancel with the $5x$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+3y=7 & |\\,\\times (-5) \\\\ 5x+4y=2\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}-5x-15y=-35 \\\\ 5x+4y=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations eliminates the $x$-variable and allows us to solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}-\\cancel{5x}-15y & =-35 \\\\ \\cancel{5x}+4y & =2 \\\\ (-15y+4y) & =(-33) \\\\ -11y & =-33 \\\\ y & =3\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can substitute $y=3$ into the equation $x+3y=7$ to solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}x+3y & =7 \\\\ x+3(3) & =7 \\\\ x+9 & =7 \\\\ x & =-2\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=-2,\\,y=3.$ So,\n\n$$\nx+y=(-2)+(3)=1.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247386 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246998 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247386 #edb/ref 1069 "q-113617" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247386 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247386 #edb/ref 1071 "Determine the value of $(\\frac{6}{2}).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247386 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a5b0a4bf-2ef4-500e-bc73-fda1cf537ad7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247386 #edb/ref 1074 "The binomial coefficient $(\\frac{n}{r})$ is given by the formula\n\n$$\n(\\frac{n}{r})=\\frac{n!}{(n-r)!\\,r!}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $n=6$ and $r=2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\frac{6}{2}) & =\\frac{6!}{(6-2)!\\cdot 2!} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\cdot 5\\cdot 4!}{4!\\cdot 2!} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\cdot 5\\cdot \\cancel{4!}}{\\cancel{4!}\\cdot 2} \\\\  & =\\frac{30}{2} \\\\  & =15.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247387 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246839 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247387 #edb/ref 1069 "q-113618" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247387 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247387 #edb/ref 1071 "Determine the value of $(\\frac{7}{5}).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247387 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b1f98a7a-f74d-5763-bfa6-f9be1199e7b5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247387 #edb/ref 1074 "The binomial coefficient $(\\frac{n}{r})$ is given by the formula\n\n$$\n(\\frac{n}{r})=\\frac{n!}{(n-r)!\\,r!}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $n=7$ and $r=5.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\frac{7}{5}) & =\\frac{7!}{(7-5)!\\cdot 5!} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\cdot 6\\cdot 5!}{2!\\cdot 5!} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\cdot 6\\cdot \\cancel{5!}}{2\\cdot \\cancel{5!}} \\\\  & =\\frac{42}{2} \\\\  & =21.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247389 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246846 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247389 #edb/ref 1069 "q-113621" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247389 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247389 #edb/ref 1071 "The coefficient of ${x}^{18}$ in the expansion of ${(2+{x}^{3})}^{10}$ is" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247389 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "521595db-fa9d-5190-ad38-7460533af950" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247389 #edb/ref 1074 "The binomial theorem states that for any positive integer $n,$ we have\n \n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}=(\\frac{n}{0}){a}^{n}{b}^{0}+(\\frac{n}{1}){a}^{n-1}{b}^{1}+(\\frac{n}{2}){a}^{n-2}{b}^{2}+…+(\\frac{n}{n}){a}^{0}{b}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn the case of ${(2+{x}^{3})}^{10},$ we have $a=2,$ $b={x}^{3},$ and $n=10.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(2+{x}^{3})}^{10}=(\\frac{10}{0}){2}^{10}{({x}^{3})}^{0}+(\\frac{10}{1}){2}^{10-1}{({x}^{3})}^{1}+(\\frac{10}{2}){2}^{10-2}{({x}^{3})}^{2}+⋯+(\\frac{10}{10}){2}^{10-10}{({x}^{3})}^{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that the ${x}^{18}$ term must be\n\n$$\n(\\frac{10}{6}){2}^{10-6}{({x}^{3})}^{6}.\n$$\n\nSimplifying this term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\frac{10}{6}){2}^{10-6}{({x}^{3})}^{6} & =210\\cdot {2}^{4}\\cdot {x}^{18} \\\\  & =210\\cdot 16\\cdot {x}^{18} \\\\  & =3\\,360{x}^{18}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coefficient of ${x}^{18}$ is $3\\,360.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247490 #edb/ref 1080 "(\\frac{3}{13},\\frac{11}{13})" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247490 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247490 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "072e45bc-d35a-54a2-8ab9-5f295ebf110f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247552 #edb/ref 1080 "2" #e