edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186249825 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186247562 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186249825 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "54362198-2ea2-5313-8e36-1d82209afe8b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186249937 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186247597 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186249937 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186247921 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186249937 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186248725 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186249937 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186249378 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186249937 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186249640 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186249937 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186249937 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186249937 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186249640 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186249937 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "94eeb897-d52c-516d-8260-943991a2821b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186249960 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186247490 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186249960 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186247608 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186249960 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186248386 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186249960 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186249109 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186249960 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186249395 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186249960 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186249960 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186249960 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186247490 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186249960 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "a5d3e168-c279-50d0-a119-0930eb63b6db" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186250247 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249825 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186250247 #edb/ref 1069 "q-114343" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186250247 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186250247 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the shortest distance between the point $(-1,5)$ and the line $y=x-2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186250247 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "633d0fd6-e7a8-521b-a27a-54658471a6ed" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186250247 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's draw a diagram describing our situation. \n\n![](images/02/021b08ba0d14e40939103d6d3e9e2004d49e5d047a5f89677b3fe542aa866f82.png)\n\n \n\nFirst, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=x-2$ and passes through the point $(-1,5).$\n\nThe given line has a slope of $1,$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $-1.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-5 & =-(x+1) \\\\ y & =-x-1+5 \\\\ y & =-x+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=x-2 \\\\ y=-x+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-2 & =-x+4 \\\\ x+x & =4+2 \\\\ 2x & =6 \\\\ x & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=3$ into either of the two equations gives $y=1.$ So, our solution is $x=3$ and $y=1.$ Therefore, the lines intersect at the point $(3,1).$\n\nFinally, we find the distance $d$ between the points $(-1,5)$ and $(3,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(3+1)^{2}+(1-5)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(4)^{2}+(-4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+16} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the shortest distance between the point $(-1,5)$ and the line $y=x-2$ is $4\\sqrt{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186250248 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249960 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186250248 #edb/ref 1069 "q-114345" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186250248 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186250248 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/ff/ff9810290868f7fd465bc310c969255b92f646d6b59ad71a93a3dcc9c38ef49c.png)\n\nFind the point on the line $3y+2x=3$ that is closest to the point $(3,5).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186250248 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "dd8e8e07-0b88-58f7-806a-13412093afb7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186250248 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we rewrite the equation of the given line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3y+2x & =3 \\\\ 3y & =-2x+3 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{2}{3}x+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=-\\frac{2}{3}x+1$ and passes through the point $(3,5).$\n\nThe given line has a slope of $-\\frac{2}{3},$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $\\frac{3}{2}.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-5 & =\\frac{3}{2}(x-3) \\\\ y & =\\frac{3}{2}x-\\frac{9}{2}+5 \\\\ y & =\\frac{3}{2}x+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=-\\frac{2}{3}x+1 \\\\ y=\\frac{3}{2}x+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{3}x+1 & =\\frac{3}{2}x+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3}x-\\frac{3}{2}x & =\\frac{1}{2}-1 \\\\ -\\frac{13}{6}x & =-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ x & =\\frac{3}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{3}{13}$ into either of the two equations gives $y=\\frac{11}{13}.$ So, our solution is $x=\\frac{3}{13}$ and $y=\\frac{11}{13}.$ So, the lines intersect at the point $(\\frac{3}{13},\\frac{11}{13}).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the point on the line  $3y+2x=3$ that is closest to the point $(3,5)$ is  $(\\frac{3}{13},\\frac{11}{13}).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186250249 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249750 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186250249 #edb/ref 1069 "q-114346" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186250249 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186250249 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/df/df3838ae44d1477a4a0de303972cd26a2ceaad9b213f1d8e4be3a53bd453aeb2.png)\n\nFind the point on the line $y-3x=2$ that is closest to the point $(4,0).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186250249 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3954acb1-fda8-5e85-a442-60c3272bf340" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186250249 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we rewrite the equation of the given line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-3x & =2 \\\\ y & =3x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=3x+2$ and passes through the point $(4,0).$\n\nThe given line has a slope of $3,$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $-\\frac{1}{3}.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-0 & =-\\frac{1}{3}(x-4) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{3}x+\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=3x+2 \\\\ y=-\\frac{1}{3}x+\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+2 & =-\\frac{1}{3}x+\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ 3x+\\frac{1}{3}x & =\\frac{4}{3}-2 \\\\ \\frac{10}{3}x & =-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ 10x & =-2 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-\\frac{1}{5}$ into either of the two equations gives $y=\\frac{7}{5}.$ So, our solution is $x=-\\frac{1}{5}$ and $y=\\frac{7}{5}.$ So, the lines intersect at the point $(-\\frac{1}{5},\\frac{7}{5}).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the point on the line  $y-3x=2$ that is closest to the point $(4,0)$ is  $(-\\frac{1}{5},\\frac{7}{5}).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186250353 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249937 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186250353 #edb/ref 1069 "q-115206" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186250353 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186250353 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following is an improper integral of the second kind?\n\n- ${\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{{x}^{2}-4}\\,$  II. ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-3}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{{x}^{2}-4}\\,$  III. ${\\int }_{-2}^{1}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{{x}^{2}-4}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186250353 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2a47dc9e-71f1-5fb5-bcb1-b46747ed60f1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186250353 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's examine each of the given integrals.\n\n- Integral I is not an improper integral since it has finite limits and the function under the integral is finite at every point $x\\in [-1,1].$\n\n- Integral II is an improper integral over an *infinite* domain since its lower limit is infinite.\n\n- Integral III is an improper integral of the second kind since it has finite limits, and \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-4}\\to -\\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to (-2)^{+}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186250876 #edb/ref 1080 "II and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186250876 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186250876 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "256aa671-a669-57d4-b91d-ced24db81e43" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186251295 #edb/ref 1080 "II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186251295 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186251295 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "5a277d68-b884-5e7d-b52b-01b0500937b3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186251536 #edb/ref 1080 "II and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186251536 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186251536 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "7725a5ac-f12f-5a08-9999-bfd41e6696a1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186251644 #edb/ref 1080 "II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186251644 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186251644 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "818adf55-7f8c-55